      "writtenOn": "2020-04-06T19:32:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This comment might be better placed at the sites of the non-interrupt-driven interrupt-handling calls.\n\nI think there might still be a race in the current code:\n1. Call syv682_handle_status_interrupt from syv682_is_vbus_present from a lower-priority task. One of the OC or OV flags is set.\n2. Read flags[port]. (Enter critical section.)\n3. ALERT_L asserts. Call syv682_handle_interrupt and read STATUS. One of the OC or OV flags is set.\n4. Read-modify-write flags[port] in response.\n5. Return from syv682_irq_deferred and suspend the task.\n6. syv682_handle_status_interrupt writes the now-stale modified value of flags[port].\n\nI think something like that would be theoretically possible as long as you read-modify-write flags[port] from different tasks. You should probably synchronize access to it.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 123,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 125,
        "endChar": 14
      },
      "revId": "e8779e4d5ee59e58fb5fda4c4d77a195d80f1a58",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e59cb025_a5c739e4",
        "filename": "driver/ppc/syv682x.c",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 125,
      "author": {
        "id": 1347775
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-06T19:58:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Good suggestion, I will add comments at the calls as well.\n\nFor this race condition you could be right, it didn\u0027t occur to me that we could need to handle an OCP interrupt in the middle of checking VBUS.\n\nI don\u0027t think it is an issue though. There is 2 main purposes of the rising-edge detection in flags:\n1. Don\u0027t spam the EC console.\n2. Don\u0027t send multiple OC events for a single OC event (this is functional, since this will permanently disable the port).\n\nI think the worst-case scenario for this situation is OC not triggered in vbus check, interrupt halts writing that to flags, OC is triggered in interrupt handler, interrupt is re-triggered. In that delay, the vbus check finishes and clears the flag. The next interrupt re-sets the flag; and we send 2 OC events instead of 1. I think we need 3 to trigger the \u0027disconnect required\u0027 state.\n\nThe \u0027race condition\u0027 I\u0027m worried about here is actually pretty specific:\n1. FRS triggered. Both the TCPC and PPC generate interrupts. \n2. The TCPC is serviced first (this is actually correct if there is a choice, since the TCPC has more time-sensitive events)\n3. The TCPC checks VBUS, thus clearing the FRS alert bit (here we should check the FRS bit, this is the race condition). I\u0027m also assuming we\u0027re using the PPC VBUS checking.\n4. We never detect an FRS event since the bit is already cleared when the PPC interrupt handler comes in.\n\nThe control4 check is not strictly necessary since none of those interrupts are actionable, but is included for coherence.",
      "parentUuid": "32f4569b_db9ae83d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 123,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 125,
        "endChar": 14
      },
      "revId": "e8779e4d5ee59e58fb5fda4c4d77a195d80f1a58",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ebc1eac6_67fadc65",
        "filename": "driver/ppc/syv682x.c",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 125,
      "author": {
        "id": 1305927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-07T17:07:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The general approach sounds appropriate.\n\nSending multiple OC events is the worst-case scenario I was thinking of as well. One other consequence could be printing multiple error messages for the same event. Neither of them are severe, but they would frustrate the two main purposes for which you added rising-edge detection. Given the I2C transactions involved in actually servicing the interrupt, synchronizing access to the flags would be fairly lightweight.\n\nIf you\u0027re leaving in a benign race condition, it would be good to call it out in the code.",
      "parentUuid": "e59cb025_a5c739e4",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 123,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 125,
        "endChar": 14
      },
      "revId": "e8779e4d5ee59e58fb5fda4c4d77a195d80f1a58",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9382eaa9_eabcff71",
        "filename": "driver/ppc/syv682x.c",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 125,
      "author": {
        "id": 1308749
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-07T17:16:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Regarding the race condition:\nWhen ALERT_L is asserted, the STATUS register is read during the deferred call, which is the HOOKS task (lowest priority). So this sequence cannot result in preemption of calls to syv682_is_vbus_present().  I suppose the it\u0027s possible that the PPC deferred interrupt could be be preempted by a TCPC interrupt that calls syv682_is_vbus_present(), since the TCPC interrupts have a separate task that is the highest priority.\n\nOf course right now, syv682_is_vbus_present() isn\u0027t used.  Because the SYV682 doesn\u0027t really support reading VBUS, maybe it\u0027s best that we explicitly disallow CONFIG_USB_PD_VBUS_DETECT_PPC in this driver?",
      "parentUuid": "e59cb025_a5c739e4",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 123,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 125,
        "endChar": 14
      },
      "revId": "e8779e4d5ee59e58fb5fda4c4d77a195d80f1a58",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e98a4605_ab9cea6b",
        "filename": "driver/ppc/syv682x.c",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 407,
      "author": {
        "id": 1308749
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-06T18:00:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think both paths should use a delay.  50ms also seems a bit long. Hard to say what the right delay is.  Based on your comments - OCP events take a few ms to clear, but OVP, RVS, and TSD events are sticky, so we\u0027ll keep handling this interrupt.\n\nFor your reference, msleep() isn\u0027t technically blocking, higher priority tasks get the opportunity to run and all tasks are higher priority than the HOOKS task. However msleep() will block any other deferred or hooks from running, so the hook_called_deferred() is the right choice.\n\nWe probably also need to consider if we need to check for a stuck OCP state, in which case we would want to turn off the port.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 407,
        "startChar": 2,
        "endLine": 407,
        "endChar": 19
      },
      "revId": "e8779e4d5ee59e58fb5fda4c4d77a195d80f1a58",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bec8171d_275e04a2",
        "filename": "driver/ppc/syv682x.c",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 407,
      "author": {
        "id": 1347775
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-06T19:58:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "True, you\u0027re right, that is my mistake. I put it at 50ms since if we aren\u0027t trying to catch FRS then nothing is really urgent, especially since all of the alerts have already been registered. is 10ms more appropriate? \n\nAnd for OCP, we do check for a stuck OCP state.  Here, the SYV682 will turn off its load switch immediately and the OCP alert will go away in a few ms. We will only continue to see overcurrent if the switch is turned back on. The pd overcurrent handler will try to turn on the load switch once or twice, if it continues to see multiple overcurrents in a certain amount of time, it will permanently disable sourcing until disconnect.",
      "parentUuid": "e98a4605_ab9cea6b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 407,
        "startChar": 2,
        "endLine": 407,
        "endChar": 19
